---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import NewsletterEmbed from '../../components/NewsletterEmbed.astro';

const articleStyles = `
    .article-body a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
    .article-body strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
    .article-body .endmatter { margin-top: 56px; padding-top: 28px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
    .article-body .endmatter-label { font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-tertiary); margin: 0 0 14px; }
    .article-body .endmatter ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 22px; }
    .article-body .endmatter li { font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-tertiary); margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 4px; }
    .article-body .endmatter li::marker { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
    .article-body .citation { font-size: .72em; vertical-align: super; margin-left: 2px; white-space: nowrap; }
    .article-body .citation a { text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; }
    .article-body ul { margin: 0 0 20px; padding-left: 20px; }
    .article-body li { font-size: 16px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .article-body li strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
    .article-body li::marker { color: var(--accent); }
    .article-body .disclaimer { margin-top: 24px; padding: 16px 18px; background: var(--bg-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-md); font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-tertiary); }
`;
---
<ArticleLayout
    title="Ozempic added 92 days to the life of mice"
    description="A Nature paper from September 2, 2026 on semaglutide and ageing. The number, the comparison with dieting, and what is still unproven."
    ogUrl="https://maxguerois.com/newsletter/glp1-longevite-souris"
    ogImage="https://maxguerois.com/health-journey/blog/glp1-longevite-souris-cover.png"
    articleStyles={articleStyles}
>
<div class="container">
    <a href="/newsletter" class="back">← Back to the newsletter</a>
    <div class="article-meta">September 6, 2026</div>
    <h1 class="article-title">Ozempic added 92 days to the life of mice</h1>
    <p class="article-subtitle">What the study says, and what it does not.</p>
    <img class="article-img" src="/health-journey/blog/glp1-longevite-souris-cover.png" alt="An Ozempic pen held above a white mouse" fetchpriority="high">

    <div class="article-body">
        <p>On September 2, <strong>Nature</strong> published a study that went around social media in 48 hours.</p>
        <p>Mice treated with semaglutide, the molecule behind Ozempic and Wegovy, lived longer than untreated mice.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        <p>It is the first signal of its kind for a molecule already sitting in pharmacies.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">The protocol</h2>
        <p>Healthy female mice, <strong>20 months</strong> old.</p>
        <p>That is late. Roughly a human in their sixties.</p>
        <p>Daily semaglutide injection, continued to the end of their lives.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">The result</h2>
        <p>Median lifespan: <strong>834 days</strong> against <strong>742</strong>.</p>
        <p>That is 92 days, or <strong>12%</strong>.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        <p>The detail that makes the number solid: causes of death were the same in both groups.</p>
        <p>Semaglutide did not remove one disease. It pushed death back on several fronts at once.</p>
        <p>That is the signature of a general slowdown of ageing, not a targeted treatment.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>

        <figure style="margin: 32px 0 28px;">
            <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10940-7/figures/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                <img src="/health-journey/blog/glp1-longevite-souris-figure.png" alt="Figure 1 of the study: survival curve and physical tests" style="width:100%; height:auto; display:block; border-radius: var(--radius-md); background:#fff;" loading="lazy">
            </a>
            <figcaption style="margin-top:10px; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; color:var(--text-tertiary);">Survival curve. Grey = treated.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 class="section-heading">The benefits</h2>
        <p>They did not just live longer. They aged better.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        <ul>
            <li>More movement and more exploration. An old mouse hugs the walls; these went back to the middle.</li>
            <li>Better spatial memory.</li>
            <li>Better coordination, more strength, more endurance.</li>
            <li>Better glucose handling.</li>
            <li>Weight loss that came from fat: percentage lean mass went up, not down.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>They also cut their own intake by 24%. Against mice held at the same calorie level, they match on strength and endurance, and do <strong>better</strong> on exploration, memory and glucose.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        <p>So the effect is not just eating less. And it comes without the permanent hunger of strict dieting.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-3">[3]</a></sup></p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">This is not an isolated signal</h2>
        <p>The study lands after several years of results pointing the same way. All of them on a GLP-1 treatment.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>SELECT</strong>: 20% fewer major cardiovascular events, in people with overweight and no diabetes. Semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly, the Wegovy dose.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-4">[4]</a></sup></li>
            <li><strong>FLOW</strong>: less kidney damage and lower mortality in type 2 diabetes. Semaglutide 1 mg weekly, the Ozempic dose.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-5">[5]</a></sup></li>
            <li>And trials under way on the liver and on Alzheimer's, again with semaglutide.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Every time, the benefit runs ahead of what weight loss alone would explain.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p>These are mice. Females only.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        <p>The mechanism behind the extra effect has not been identified.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-2">[2]</a></sup></p>
        <p>And nothing is proven in humans on longevity. Trials are starting; it will take years.<sup class="citation"><a href="#source-2">[2]</a></sup></p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">What I take from it</h2>
        <p>"Ozempic makes you live longer" runs too fast. But the opposite would be just as wrong.</p>
        <p>It is the first time a molecule already prescribed to millions reproduces, in a mammal, what only food deprivation could do.</p>
        <p>I increasingly think we are looking at a class of molecules that goes far beyond weight loss. GLP-1, or whatever comes after it.</p>

        <div style="margin: var(--sp-12) 0;"><NewsletterEmbed /></div>

        <div class="endmatter">
            <p class="endmatter-label">Sources</p>
            <ol>
                <li id="source-1">Feng, Y. et al., <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10940-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Late-life semaglutide treatment slows ageing and extends lifespan in female mice</a>, <em>Nature</em>, September 2, 2026.</li>
                <li id="source-2">Lenharo, M., <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-02757-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Can GLP-1 drugs slow ageing? Mouse study shows promise</a>, <em>Nature News</em>, September 2, 2026.</li>
                <li id="source-3">Fontana, L., Partridge, L. &amp; Longo, V. D., <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20395504/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Extending healthy life span: from yeast to humans</a>, <em>Science</em>, 2010.</li>
                <li id="source-4">Lincoff, A. M. et al., <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37952131/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semaglutide and cardiovascular outcomes in obesity without diabetes</a> (SELECT trial), <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2023.</li>
                <li id="source-5">Perkovic, V. et al., <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38785209/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Effects of semaglutide on chronic kidney disease in patients with type 2 diabetes</a> (FLOW trial), <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2024.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="disclaimer">General information, not medical advice. This study was done in mice and does not justify any treatment decision.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
</ArticleLayout>
